PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C. None of the above is correct.

The three experiments together show a consistent pattern: at the matched 10 mg/kg dose, anti-TNF–GRM produces faster and more complete resolution of FITC-induced ear swelling (0.02 mm vs 0.30 mm) and arthritis paw swelling (reversed by day 7, −0.1 mm) than anti-TNF alone, while also limiting bone-density loss to −0.3 mm³ versus −0.75 mm³ at day 14. These outcomes directly contradict every statement that asserts equal osteoporosis risk (B, D, H), reversed efficacy ordering (A), or unsupported claims that GRM lacks anti-inflammatory activity (G).  

Options F and I further extrapolate GRM performance to an untested 10 mg/kg dose, which the study never evaluated. Most critically, E and I claim the chosen doses permit a clean head-to-head comparison of efficacy and side effects across all agents. Yet Experiment 3 deliberately tested GRM at only 3 mg/kg while holding the antibody arms at 10 mg/kg; this intentional mismatch prevents any reliable inference about whether conjugation itself alters the GRM safety margin at equivalent exposure. Because that design choice leaves the therapeutic-index comparison incomplete, the assertion that “dosage was chosen correctly” cannot be sustained. Consequently every listed statement contains at least one factual reversal or unsupported extrapolation, leaving only C consistent with the reported measurements.}

